Q: Pass c# byte[] to c++ unsigned char* A: <p>Just &quot;pin&quot; the object so it would not be moved around by the garbage collector during the moment it is used by the external C++ code.</p>
<p>I would suggest you try the following code snippet:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>fixed (var msg = Enumerable.Range(1, 100).Cast&lt;byte&gt;().ToArray())
{
    DllAccess.AddMessage(msg);
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Automatic pinning</strong></p>
<p>Since it's quite fundamental for the data to be pinned on C#'s side before it can be passed to C++ code, the <code>fixed</code> code structure becomes a minor boilerplate. To reduce the ritual doing it, C# will do automatic pinning, provided that you declare the P/invoke signature correctly.</p>
<p>So, instead of:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>static class DllAccess
{
  [DllImport(&quot;lib.dll&quot;, CallingConvention = CallingConvention.Cdecl )]
  public static extern int AddMessage(byte[] msg);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Here's the signature which denotes its argument should be automatically pinned when the function is called: (<code>[In, Out]</code> is added)</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>static class DllAccess
{
  [DllImport(&quot;lib.dll&quot;, CallingConvention = CallingConvention.Cdecl )]
  public static extern int AddMessage([In, Out] byte[] msg);
}
</code></pre>
<p>With that revised signature in place, every time when <code>AddMessage()</code> is called, GC will not move its argument, even without the <code>fixed {...}</code> block.</p>
<p><strong>Blitable or nonblitable, that is the question</strong></p>
<p>Calling P/invoke function with blitable data is always automatically pinned. Nonblitable, no. <code>byte</code> is blitable, but <code>byte[]</code> is not, because it is a C# array object, and object cannot be blitable.</p>
<p>Interesting, if we change the P/invoke signature, it becomes blitable:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>static class DllAccess
{
  [DllImport(&quot;lib.dll&quot;, CallingConvention = CallingConvention.Cdecl )]
  public static extern int AddMessage(byte[] msg, int length);
}
</code></pre>
<p>This is because the code signature follows a standard way to pass a byte array between C# &amp; C++. C++ would accept the first argument as the beginning of bytes, and the second argument the length of the bytes. In the C# side, the caller will also pass the address of the beginning of the first byte in the array, not the array object itself, and then you need to pass the length of its elements. Hence, the C# caller would automatically pin in before the entrance to C++ code and immediately unpin it on the exit.</p>
<p>This solution, however, would require the C++ code to be changed to accept the <code>length</code> argument, which is actually a better solution. Without, C++ code will have no clue how to determine the size of the array.</p>
<p>See Microsoft's document on blitable/nonblitable data types:</p>
<p><a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/framework/interop/blittable-and-non-blittable-types" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/framework/interop/blittable-and-non-blittable-types</a></p>

Q: Get a column of values from a 2d array A: <p>Here's an example. (It's probably not as "elegant" as xdazz", but it's an alternate without using "use" closure.)  This is how the array might start out like:</p>

<p><strong>Start</strong></p>

<pre><code>array (size=2)
  0 =&gt; 
    array (size=1)
      'woeid' =&gt; string '56413072' (length=8)
  1 =&gt; 
    array (size=1)
      'woeid' =&gt; string '56412936' (length=8)      
</code></pre>

<p>Then you might want to have something like this:</p>

<p><strong>Target</strong></p>

<pre><code>   array (size=2)
      0 =&gt; string '56413072' (length=8)
      1 =&gt; string '56412936' (length=8)
</code></pre>

<p>You can use <a href="http://www.php.net/manual/en/function.array-walk-recursive.php" rel="nofollow">array_walk_recursive</a></p>

<p><strong>Code</strong></p>

<pre><code>$woeid = array();
array_walk_recursive($result['results']['Result'], function ($item, $key, $woeid) {
     if ($key == 'woeid') {                                       
           $woeid[] = $item;
     }
}, &amp;$woeid);
</code></pre>

Q: Memory limits on Arduino A: <p>Add this function and call it in <code>setup()</code> every now and then in your loop to make sure RAM is not being used up.</p>
<pre><code>// Private function: from http://arduino.cc/playground/Code/AvailableMemory  
int freeRam () {
  extern int __heap_start, *__brkval; 
  int v; 
  return (int) &amp;v - (__brkval == 0 ? (int) &amp;__heap_start : (int) __brkval); 
}
</code></pre>
<p>You need to call it for example inside a print:
Serial.println(freeRam());</p>
